---
id: PY-008
type: rule
family: python-reliability
title: eval() / exec() / compile() called with a dynamic argument
version: 1

severity: error
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 8

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.py]
allowlist: ['**/test_*.py', '**/*_test.py', '**/tests/**', '**/conftest.py', '**/explain/**']

detection:
  type: regex
  # RE2 has no lookahead, so "identifier that is not a string prefix" is
  # expressed positively. Static (no fire): bare quotes, and r/b/u prefixes in
  # any order or case (`r`, `b`, `u`, `rb`, `br`, `Rb`, `BR`, ...) — an
  # identifier followed immediately by a quote can only be a prefixed literal.
  # Dynamic (fire): `*`/`**` unpacking; any f-carrying prefix (`f`, `rf`, `fr`,
  # and case variants) since f-strings interpolate at run time; and an
  # identifier terminated by anything that is not an identifier char or a
  # quote — which covers `,` `)` `(` `.` `[` but also operators (`+`, `%`,
  # `**`, `:=`), keyword operators reached across a space (`if`, `and`, `or`,
  # `not`, `is`, `in`, `await`), keyword arguments, and non-ASCII identifier
  # bytes. `$` catches a call whose first argument ends the line (wrapped
  # calls); the scanner matches per line, so nothing else reaches them.
  # Do NOT narrow the terminator to an allowlist of punctuation: that silently
  # drops every operator form, which is the canonical injection shape.
  #
  # Receiver gate (CIB-332): `compile` alone is dot-gated — it matches only at
  # start of line or after a byte that is not an identifier char or `.`, so a
  # dotted receiver (`re.compile`, `torch.compile`, `self.compile`,
  # `Pattern.compile`) is not the builtin and does not fire, while unqualified
  # `compile(src, "<s>", "exec")` still does. `eval` and `exec` keep `\b` on
  # purpose: `df.eval(expr)` and an `obj.exec(cmd)` wrapper are genuine
  # execution surfaces, so attribute access on them must still fire.
  # The gate consumes its one leading byte (same construct as PY-006's
  # `(^|[^.\w])print\s*\(`), so a non-start-of-line `compile` finding reports a
  # column one to the left; `column` is dropped by the JSON renderer and SARIF
  # emits line-only, so only the miette caret sees it.
  pattern: '(?:\b(?:eval|exec)|(?:^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.])compile)\s*\(\s*(?:\*|(?:[fF][rR]?|[rR][fF])[''"]|[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:[^A-Za-z0-9_''"]|$))'

related: [AP-008, PY-009]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

Don't call `eval()`, `exec()`, or `compile(..., "exec")` with a dynamic
argument. An identifier as the first argument means the string being executed
is not a static literal and may be attacker-controlled at runtime.

Replace with a structured dispatch — a dict of callables keyed on the input, a
pydantic / attrs schema for data validation, or a restricted interpreter
(`ast.literal_eval` for literals only, RestrictedPython, a subprocess with a
clean env) if you genuinely need to run untrusted code.

A bare string literal you control end-to-end (`eval("1 + 1")`) or a raw, bytes,
or unicode prefixed literal (`re.compile(r'^\d+$')`, `compile(rb"...", "<s>",
"exec")`, `eval(u"1")`) does not fire. f-strings do fire in every prefix
ordering (`f`, `rf`, `fr`) — they interpolate run-time values.

Only unqualified `compile(...)` is the builtin, so a dotted receiver
(`re.compile(pattern_var)`, `torch.compile(model)`, `self.compile(src)`) does
not fire whatever the argument is. `eval` and `exec` have no such exemption:
`df.eval(expr)` and `obj.exec(cmd)` still fire, because a method spelled `eval`
or `exec` is a real execution surface.

A literal first argument is **not** proof the call is safe: the rule reads only
the first token after `(` on a single line, so a formatted or concatenated
literal (`exec("DELETE FROM %s" % table)`, `eval("{}".format(x))`,
`eval("x" + user)`), a parenthesised expression, and a call whose argument
starts on a later line are blind spots you must review by hand.

## Why It's Harmful

`eval()`, `exec()`, and `compile()` turn a run-time string into code in the
process's full privilege. When the first argument is an identifier, static
analysis cannot see the payload — it may be attacker-controlled.

Raw, bytes, and unicode prefixes (`r`, `b`, `u`, and two-letter combinations
such as `rb`/`br`, in any case) are treated as static, the same as a bare
quote, so `re.compile(r'^\d+$')`, `compile(rb"...", "<s>", "exec")`, and
`eval(u"1")` do not fire. Everything else does: f-strings in any ordering
(`exec(f"{user_input}")`, `eval(rf"{user_input}")`), plain identifiers
(`eval(user_input)`, `eval(user_input.strip())`, `eval(user_input[0])`),
composed payloads (`eval(prefix + user_input)`, `exec(tmpl % vars)`,
`eval(cmd if trusted else default)`), keyword arguments
(`compile(source=src, filename="<s>", mode="exec")`), and unpacking
(`eval(*args)`).

`compile` is also an ordinary method name, so the rule reads it as the builtin
only when it is unqualified. A dotted receiver — `re.compile(pattern_var)`,
`torch.compile(model)`, `self.compile(src)`, `Pattern.compile(name)` — is
exempt, which is what makes the named-constant regex idiom (`DATE_RE = r"..."`
then `re.compile(DATE_RE)`) clean. That exemption is `compile`-only:
`builtins.eval(user_input)`, `df.eval(expr)`, and `obj.exec(cmd)` all still
fire.
